  "analysis": "Market regime: a clear risk-off rotation is underway. Over the last 20 sessions, growth/tech (A11 -9.3%, A01 -26.1%), emerging (A12 -7.9%) and energy (A03 -7.4%, A13 -5.5%) have sold off sharply, while defensive/value areas — health (A04 +11.3%, A07 +22.8%), financials (A08 +6.3%, A15 +3.2%), bonds (A09 +3.6%) and intl developed (A06 +0.9%) — have held up or rallied. Gold (A05 -3.2%) is also soft. The forecast intervals confirm this: the narrowest 5-day intervals belong to A09 (3.96%), A08 (5.36%), A06 (5.92%) and A04 (5.98%) — the most predictable assets — while the widest belong to A01 (22.7%), A14 (11.6%), A07 (11.4%), A12 (10.5%) and A11 (10.1%). Since the median forecast has no directional skill, I position based on interval width (risk) and the observed trend, not on median sign. Main risks: (1) momentum reversal in health/financials after their strong run; (2) concentration if the rotation stalls — health and financials groups stay at 15.1% and 18.1%, well under the 30% cap; (3) the four zeroed assets could bounce, but their downtrends, high vol and wide intervals make the baseline weights unattractive; (4) equity exposure remains ~88%, appropriate given the small -1.6% drawdown and no drawdown actions. Changes from the current portfolio: keep zeros in A01, A12, A03, A13; trim A11 (tech ETF) from 4.5% to 2.5% on its downtrend and wide interval; trim A06 from 13.0% to 10.0% to reduce single-ETF concentration while staying overweight vs baseline given its best-in-class risk profile; add to A04 (9.7%→12.0%) and A08 (9.9%→12.0%), which combine positive momentum with the narrowest equity intervals so downside is bounded; keep A09 bonds at 15.9% as the defensive anchor; cash rises modestly to 11.8% as dry powder.",
